# fuse3 -- libfuse and, far more importantly, the setuid fusermount3 helper.
#
# Packaged for two consumers that need different halves of it:
#
#   flatpak   links libfuse3 (revokefs-fuse, and the document portal's
#             /run/user/<uid>/doc mount). Its meson takes fuse3 or fuse2 and
#             prefers fuse3, so there is no reason to package fuse2.
#
#   AppImage  needs NO library from this package at all. The current type-2
#             runtime is statically linked against musl in an Alpine chroot --
#             upstream's own words are "since the runtime is linked statically,
#             libfuse2 is no longer required on the target system". What it does
#             need is a setuid-root fusermount* on $PATH, which its runtime.c
#             locates by walking $PATH for a name matching "fusermount" plus
#             optional digits and checking the setuid bit. fusermount3 satisfies
#             that, so this single package is the whole of AppImage support.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/libfuse/libfuse/releases/download/fuse-3.16.2/fuse-3.16.2.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=f797055d9296b275e981f5f62d4e32e089614fc253d1ef2985851025b8a0ce87
SRC_DIR=fuse-3.16.2

# EVERY OPTION BELOW WAS READ FROM meson_options.txt AND util/meson.build IN THIS
# TARBALL, with its upstream default recorded beside it. A misspelled meson
# option is not an error -- it is accepted and silently ignored -- so an option
# left unnamed is indistinguishable from an option nobody considered, and
# writing the default down is the only evidence that a decision happened.
#
# -Duseroot=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE. This is not an optimisation and the
# reason is stronger than "it chowns things". util/install_helper.sh, which meson
# runs via add_install_script, does this when useroot is true:
#
#     chown root:root "${DESTDIR}${bindir}/fusermount3"
#     chmod u+s       "${DESTDIR}${bindir}/fusermount3"
#     if test ! -e "${DESTDIR}/dev/fuse"; then
#         mkdir -p "${DESTDIR}/dev"
#         mknod "${DESTDIR}/dev/fuse" -m 0666 c 10 229
#     fi
#
# Two problems, and the second is the one that would have been hard to find. The
# chown needs root and the build is unprivileged. And the mknod would put a REAL
# DEVICE NODE INSIDE THE PACKAGE -- Duct mounts devtmpfs on /dev, which creates
# /dev/fuse itself once the module is loaded, so a packaged /dev/fuse is both
# useless and a file this package would then own. install.sh sets the mode
# itself, after the strip, and asserts it.
#
# -Dtests=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE: the test files are compiled on every
# build otherwise, for tests nothing here runs.
#
# -Dexamples=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE. The passthrough demos are not
# something a distribution ships.
#
# -Dinitscriptdir= empty. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS '/etc/init.d/', and the option's
# own description says "if empty, disable init script installation" -- so empty
# is the documented way to switch it off, not a guess. Duct has no sysvinit;
# busybox init runs duct-live's rc.
#
# -Dsysconfdir=/etc. THIS ONE IS NOT IN THE OPTIONS FILE AND IS EASY TO MISS.
# util/meson.build computes
#     fuseconf_path = join_paths(get_option('prefix'), get_option('sysconfdir'), 'fuse.conf')
# and compiles it into fusermount3 as -DFUSE_CONF. _scripts/build-meson.sh passes
# --prefix=/usr and nothing else, and meson's default sysconfdir is the RELATIVE
# 'etc', so without this the config lands at /usr/etc/fuse.conf and the binary is
# compiled to look for it there. Nothing fails; the file is simply in a place no
# administrator will find.
#
# -Dudevrulesdir=/usr/lib/udev/rules.d. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS '' (empty), which
# util/meson.build resolves by querying udev's pkg-config udevdir -- and if that
# lookup fails it emits `warning('could not determine udevdir, udev.rules will
# not be installed')` and carries on. A warning, not an error, and the rule it
# skips is what sets the mode on /dev/fuse for a non-root user. Naming the path
# removes the lookup entirely.
#
# The path is /usr/lib and not /lib for a PACKAGING reason rather than a runtime
# one. eudev is configured --with-rootprefix= empty so it reads /lib/udev/rules.d,
# which is the same directory because duct-filesystem ships /lib as a symlink to
# usr/lib. But a package must not stage into /lib: duct-filesystem owns that
# symlink, and tape's conflict check exempts directories (install.go skips
# entries where IsDir) while rejecting two packages owning one file -- and a
# symlink is a file. pkgs/linux/install.sh hit exactly this with its modules.
#
# NOT SET, DELIBERATELY: -Dutils. It defaults to true and MUST STAY TRUE.
# util/meson.build line 3 is `executable('fusermount3', ...)`, so utils is what
# builds fusermount3 and mount.fuse3. Turning it off leaves a libfuse with no
# helper that builds cleanly, installs cleanly, and removes the entire reason
# this package exists for AppImage. Recorded as an instruction not to, because
# an absent flag is invisible and "why is this not disabled like its neighbours"
# is exactly what a later tidy-up will ask.
MESON_ARGS="-Duseroot=false -Dexamples=false -Dtests=false -Dinitscriptdir= \
-Dsysconfdir=/etc -Dudevrulesdir=/usr/lib/udev/rules.d"
